so yesterday five percent gdp really  looks like confirmation of what we've  been talking about all fall and winter  which is this divergence the US economy  powering ahead while the rest of the  world is slowing down what are the  consequences of divergence for emerging  markets so the major consequences of  divergence for emerging markets is that  monetary policy in the advanced world  will go in different directions the US  will tighten Europe will continue to  loosen and Japan will be uber loose so  exchange rates currencies are going to  move and the emerging world is going to  have to navigate a lot more currency  volatility I talked to me a bit about  Russia yesterday S&amp;P said its sovereign  rating is on the brink of junk obviously  Vladimir Putin is doing a lot of things  to try to rescue the sell-off in the  ruble how does Russia get through the  winter here so first of all it will be  downgraded to junk simply because they  don't have enough reserves to meet all  the requirements okay and it's not just  protecting their creditworthiness its  meeting external debt is slowing down  capital outflows it is stabilizing the  currency it is meeting input demands  when their exports have been cut by half  so 400 billion may sound like a lot but  it's not enough for these multiple  objectives so something is going to have  to give and I think a couple of things  are going to give one is the credit  rating and secondly controls I think  what what is looking at is recession  inflation periodic pressure on the  currency and mr. Putin having to make a  very important decision as to whether to  re-engage with the West or not
 

